WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.826 --> 00:00:09.700
JIMMY TRAN: Hi there.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.455
Jimmy here.

00:00:10.455 --> 00:00:12.580
HEATHER CROSS: Always good
to have you back, Jimmy.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.496
In today's episode, we're
going to be focusing

00:00:14.496 --> 00:00:16.059
on different Cloud Functions.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:17.809
JIMMY TRAN: When
building apps, developers

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:21.070
rely on various cloud services,
including storage, messaging,

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.560
data analytics, and
mobile development.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.290
As developers expand their
use, seamless integration

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.570
between these services
can be a challenge.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.690
HEATHER CROSS: Very true.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.049
So today we'll introduce
Google Cloud Functions,

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.150
a solution that allows
you to connect services

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.490
together and extend their
behavior just by adding code.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.150
We'll also run through a quick
demo of a self-paced lab that

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.910
will allow you to
create, deploy, and test

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.750
a Cloud Function.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.220
JIMMY TRAN: Cloud Functions,
sounds pretty generic to me.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.900
So what is it really?

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.580
Google Cloud Functions
can be summarized

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.040
as a serverless compute
solution for creating

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.360
event-driven applications.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.120
HEATHER CROSS: So
let's define an event.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.120
An event could be something like
a change to data in a database

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.520
or files added to
a storage system.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.940
You can create a response to
that event with a trigger.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.570
A trigger is an
indication that you're

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.510
interested in a certain
event or set of events.

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:16.160
Adding a Cloud
Function to the trigger

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.665
allows you to respond
to that event.

00:01:17.665 --> 00:01:19.790
JIMMY TRAN: Because the
software and infrastructure

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:23.530
are fully managed by Google, you
only need to provide the code.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.530
A function can scale from a few
requests to millions per day

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:28.790
without any work from you.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:31.040
HEATHER CROSS: Click here
to view a great introduction

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.097
by our Product
Manager, Jason Polites,

00:01:33.097 --> 00:01:35.430
about building serverless
applications with Google Cloud

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.170
Functions.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.800
JIMMY TRAN: So what can you
do with Cloud Functions?

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.851
Let's look at some examples
we can all relate to.

00:01:45.851 --> 00:01:47.475
HEATHER CROSS: All
right, I've got one.

00:01:47.475 --> 00:01:50.790
You know when you
subscribe to a newsletter--

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.530
Hogwarts number one fan--

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.300
and you magically get an
email delivered to your inbox?

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.092
Yep, that's a trigger
for a Cloud Function.

00:01:57.092 --> 00:01:59.008
JIMMY TRAN: Developers
can also take advantage

00:01:59.008 --> 00:02:02.040
of Cloud Functions to offload
resource-intensive work that

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.620
wouldn't be practical to
run on a user's device.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.330
An example could be
to write a function

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.360
that upon upload of an
image to a storage bucket

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.970
a thumbnail of the image
is created and then saved

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.720
in another bucket.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.440
HEATHER CROSS: That's
another great example, Jimmy.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:14.940
JIMMY TRAN: Thank you.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.600
HEATHER CROSS: And
of course, there

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.058
are many opportunities
to integrate

00:02:17.058 --> 00:02:19.200
with third-party
services and APIs, too.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.057
JIMMY TRAN: Now to Qwiklabs--

00:02:26.057 --> 00:02:27.890
the Cloud Functions lab
is available for use

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.560
with the command line, as well
as through the Google Cloud

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.880
Platform console.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.640
You can check out the links
to start the Qwiklabs here.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.181
HEATHER CROSS: In
both labs, you will

00:02:36.181 --> 00:02:38.435
create a Cloud Function,
deploy and test the function,

00:02:38.435 --> 00:02:40.280
and view logs.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.780
JIMMY TRAN: So far
in the lab, we've

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.650
created a Cloud Function
based on an HTTP trigger

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.360
and deployed it.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.500
Once deployed, you
can test a function,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.380
provide a triggering event,
in this case, a simple "hello

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.420
world" message.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.240
You can see the success of the
tested function in the output

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.900
field.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.070
You can easily see the
logs for your function.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.029
Well, there we have it.

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:07.486
Thanks for watching
our quick video

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:08.570
on Google Cloud Functions.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.027
HEATHER CROSS:
